bin/
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
# Anchored to the repo root so this ignores only a stray binary built there
# (e.g. `go build` with no -o); without the leading slash the bare name would
# also match the cmd/tierd SOURCE dir, silently dropping any new file added
# under it. Built binaries normally land in bin/ (ignored above).
/tierd
# Local Claude Code / agent workspace state.
#
# Scoped deliberately (was a blanket `.claude/`): the blanket rule meant ZERO of
# the 25 agent definitions were tracked, so a .claude wipe, a bad `git clean` or
# a fresh clone lost every one of them with no recovery path — and the CLAUDE.md
# review matrix depends on those agents existing. Agent definitions, skills,
# agent memory and the agent manifest are project configuration and belong in
# the repo; only genuinely machine-local state stays out.
.claude/settings.local.json
.claude/worktrees/
.claude/**/node_modules/

# The Model Report instrument is its own repo (tiermetric/model-bench).
# Never vendor it into tier — an errant `git add -A` did exactly that once.
model-bench/

# >>> tool-managed: agent durability >>>
.claude/agents/*.md
.claude/agent-memory-local/
.claude/settings.local.json
.claude/worktrees/
# <<< tool-managed: agent durability <<<

# Re-include the agent BODIES, overriding the tool-managed block above.
# That block ignores .claude/agents/*.md on the theory that the agent manifest
# makes them reproducible. It does not: the manifest is 8K of metadata (name,
# model, scope, version) against 284K of actual system prompts, so it records
# WHICH agents should exist and nothing about what they say. Bodies are
# recoverable only from the coordination server, which makes 25 agent definitions —
# and with them the CLAUDE.md review matrix — dependent on a service rather
# than on the repo. Track them. (Steve, 2026-07-23.)
!.claude/agents/*.md

# Compiled docgen artifact. The Makefile only ever runs `go run .`, so this is
# always a stray `go build` -- but it is 6.6MB of Mach-O embedding Go
# module-cache paths under the developer's home directory, and it sat untracked
# and UNIGNORED in the tree while a publish export was being built (#580).
# Ignoring it stops `git add -A` making the leak permanent; publish-audit gate
# (i) is the backstop that stops it shipping.
tools/docgen/docgen
